Title: Zassenhaus Lemma/Lemma 2

Let $G$ be a [[Definition:Group|group]] whose [[Definition:Identity Element|identity]] is $e$.

Let $H_1, H_2$ be [[Definition:Subgroup|subgroups]] of $G$.

Let:
{{begin-eqn}}
{{eqn | l = N_1
      | o = \lhd
      | r = H_1
}}
{{eqn | l = N_2
      | o = \lhd
      | r = H_2
}}
{{end-eqn}}

where $\lhd$ denotes the relation of being a [[Definition:Normal Subgroup|normal subgroup]].

Then:
:$\paren {H_1 \cap N_2} \paren {H_2 \cap N_1} \lhd \paren {H_1 \cap H_2}$

proof_numbered:
0001 | Consider arbitrary $x_h \in H_1 \cap H_2$.
0002 | 
0003 | By definition of [[Definition:Set Intersection|set intersection]], it follows that:
0004 | :$x_h \in H_1 \land x_h \in H_2$
0005 | 
0006 | We aim to show that:
0007 | :$x_h \paren {H_1 \cap N_2} \paren {H_2 \cap N_1} x_h^{-1} = \paren {H_1 \cap N_2} \paren {H_2 \cap N_1}$
0008 | 
0009 | thus demonstrating:
0010 | :$\paren {H_1 \cap N_2} \paren {H_2 \cap N_1} \lhd \paren {H_1 \cap H_2}$ 
0011 | by the [[Normal Subgroup Test]].
0012 | 
0013 | First note that from [[Intersection with Normal Subgroup is Normal]]:
0014 | 
0015 | :$H_1 \cap N_2 \lhd H_1$
0016 | :$H_2 \cap N_1 \lhd H_2$
0017 | 
0018 | We have:
0019 | 
0020 | {{begin-eqn}}
0021 | {{eqn | l = x_h \paren {H_1 \cap N_2} \paren {H_2 \cap N_1} x_h^{-1}
0022 |       | r = x_h \paren {H_1 \cap N_2} {x_h}^{-1} x_h \paren {H_2 \cap N_1} x_h^{-1}
0023 |       | c = 
0024 | }}
0025 | {{eqn | r = x_h \paren {H_1 \cap N_2} \paren { {x_h}^{-1} x_h} \paren {H_2 \cap N_1} x_h^{-1}
0026 |       | c = {{Group-axiom|1}}
0027 | }}
0028 | {{eqn | r = x_h \paren {H_1 \cap N_2} e \paren {H_2 \cap N_1} x_h^{-1}
0029 |       | c = {{Defof|Inverse Element}}
0030 | }}
0031 | {{eqn | r = x_h \paren {H_1 \cap N_2} \paren {H_2 \cap N_1} x_h^{-1}
0032 |       | c = {{Defof|Identity Element}}
0033 | }}
0034 | {{eqn | r = \paren {H_1 \cap N_2} x_h \paren {H_2 \cap N_1} x_h^{-1}
0035 |       | c = as $H_1 \cap N_2 \lhd H_1$
0036 | }}
0037 | {{eqn | r = \paren {H_1 \cap N_2} \paren {H_2 \cap N_1}
0038 |       | c = as $H_2 \cap N_1 \lhd H_2$
0039 | }}
